WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680
The first film showed why sex cells carry half the chromosomes.

2
00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.420
But if every sex cell gets half, why are those halves different?

3
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.000
Return to the blue and gold chromosome pairs.

4
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:16.140
This time, follow which member enters each sex cell.

5
00:00:19.567 --> 00:00:21.507
A gene can have different versions.

6
00:00:22.947 --> 00:00:24.127
Each version is an allele.

7
00:00:25.487 --> 00:00:29.827
Put capital B on the blue chromosome and lowercase b on the gold one.

8
00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:33.907
A sex cell receives only one member of this pair.

9
00:00:35.207 --> 00:00:39.947
Each sex cell has an equal chance of getting capital B or lowercase b.

10
00:00:40.687 --> 00:00:43.767
Across many sex cells, about half carry each.

11
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:46.380
Now restore three pairs.

12
00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:52.780
During meiosis, each pair sorts by chance, independently of the other pairs in this

13
00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:56.580
model. The blue member may move left in one pair.

14
00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.360
In another pair, the gold member may move left.

15
00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:03.400
Each choice changes the final mix.

16
00:01:04.383 --> 00:01:08.123
One green sex cell now holds a blue, gold, blue pattern.

17
00:01:09.383 --> 00:01:11.943
Reset the parent cell and turn each pair again.

18
00:01:13.283 --> 00:01:15.203
Must the next sex cell match that pattern?

19
00:01:16.543 --> 00:01:18.903
Predict what the independent choices will do.

20
00:01:24.817 --> 00:01:26.517
The next cell need not match.

21
00:01:27.777 --> 00:01:30.577
A gold, blue, gold pattern is also possible.

22
00:01:31.957 --> 00:01:36.197
Each pair still contributes one chromosome, so every sex cell has half.

23
00:01:37.477 --> 00:01:39.397
What changes is which member arrives.

24
00:01:40.777 --> 00:01:44.617
Different eggs and sperm can therefore carry different mixes of alleles.

25
00:01:45.617 --> 00:01:48.817
Chance does not promise exact halves in a small group.

26
00:01:49.937 --> 00:01:52.757
A short run can contain more copies of one allele.

27
00:01:54.137 --> 00:01:58.137
Across many model sex cells, the results should approach equal shares.

28
00:01:59.557 --> 00:02:03.317
An allele is not stronger because it appears a little more often once.

29
00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:08.670
Blue and gold mark two pair members, not stronger and weaker chromosomes.

30
00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:12.290
This model follows whole chromosomes only.

31
00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:15.870
In high school, you will add crossing over.

32
00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:21.590
Paired chromosomes can exchange matching pieces, creating still more allele

33
00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:26.570
combinations. That later detail does not change today's sorting rule.

34
00:02:27.633 --> 00:02:29.653
Mitosis does a different job.

35
00:02:30.953 --> 00:02:35.433
It keeps the chromosome number and makes matching body cells for growth and repair.

36
00:02:36.933 --> 00:02:40.313
Meiosis makes sex cells with half and can make different mixes.

37
00:02:41.793 --> 00:02:47.593
Most asexual reproduction uses the copy path; sexual reproduction uses the sorting

38
00:02:47.593 --> 00:02:48.593
path.

39
00:02:49.017 --> 00:02:51.097
Now the sibling puzzle has an answer.

40
00:02:52.417 --> 00:02:56.997
Each child begins from a different egg and sperm, usually carrying different allele

41
00:02:56.997 --> 00:03:01.837
mixes. The alleles stay separate rather than blending like paint.

42
00:03:03.457 --> 00:03:08.717
Sexual reproduction: meiosis halves chromosome number and makes mixes; fertilization

43
00:03:08.717 --> 00:03:09.617
restores pairs.
